despite all our current knowledge and  technology all that we understand and  can do up to roughly 67 percent of the  earth's surface to this day remains  unexplored and unseen by human eyes  around 71 percent of earth's surface is  covered by ocean most of us know this we  know that the ocean is very big but when  you realize that up to potentially 95 of  it is still unexplored unmapped and  unseen you also realize that the ocean  is not only huge it is disturbingly  unknown  there is a specific type of phobia known  as the lassophobia which is the intense  fear of deep bodies of water like the  ocean and sea  for some the mere sight of underwater  images can be enough to severely trigger  this phobia for others the feeling might  only be felt when physically swimming in  deep water or out on a boat in the  middle of the ocean  like most phobias there is variance in  the intensity and forms experienced  among sufferers arguably however the  more one learns about the ocean the more  thalassophobia and at least some acute  form is likely to reveal itself in most  of us  being fearful of a beastly eerie state  of matter mostly steeped in darkness  that you are not built to survive in  where dangerous things loom around that  are built to thrive in it doomed to have  your life taken from you by merely going  too deep or staying in too long can just  make a lot of sense  the average depth of the ocean is about  2.3 miles or 3700 meters keyword however  is average the deepest parts of the  ocean run much much deeper deeper than  most of us are likely aware and deeper  than most of us can likely comprehend  taking a brief journey into its depths  starting a bit closer to the surface at  500 meters beneath it we find ourselves  in the middle of what is referred to as  the twilight zone  for reference at this depth if you stood  at the very top of the tip of the one  world trade center the tallest building  in the united states and look down  that's about how deep you would be  submerged at this depth  this is about the maximum depth that  modern nuclear attack submarines  typically travel the deepest most  non-research vessels can traverse  without risking literally being imploded  by the weight of the water pressure  here light rapidly dissipates with each  meter deeper we go and amidst the  increasing darkness we find strange and  unsettling creatures  among which are sperm whales hunting  battling and feeding on giant and  colossal squids all as big as the size  of houses  even more frightening despite their  massive size we didn't even know that  colossal squids existed until 1925  also in this area are otherworldly  bioluminescent creatures that produce  and emit light as a means of hunting and  avoiding being hunted  even more unsettling than what we do  know about this area however is what we  don't a large number of various types of  organisms in this zone remain mostly a  mystery given their elusiveness and the  difficult conditions for research  activity  if we go two times deeper five hundred  more meters at around 1 000 meters under  the surface we reach the midnight zone  this depth would be the equivalent  distance of stacking more than seven of  the great pyramids of giza on top of  each other  at this point light from the surface no  longer reaches here and it is pitch  black  light that traveled around 93 million  miles or about 150 billion meters from  the sun through space through earth's  atmosphere and onto earth's surface at  just one thousand meters or a little  over half a mile under the ocean it has  met its match and been killed off in  entirety  the murkiness and darkness of the water  here is so extreme that massive objects  like sunken ships and airplanes even  when illuminated by powerful lights can  only be seen in mere fractions of tens  of feet and yet in this void life still  lurks  creatures in this zone survive and  thrive in complete darkness under  extreme water pressure with no light  hostile co